{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791793378,"nonce":"weWKWPbDE2GovDb1SNZxN/jOJvA=","title":"Typing macros through secondary module paths and nested static calls remain unexpanded","message":"TestType calls MyMacro.MyMacroHelper.followWithAbstracts. MacroRegistry registers unit.MyMacroHelper.followWithAbstracts but has no alias for the containing MyMacro module, so lowering leaves a runtime call to an uncompiled macro trap. The same behavior occurs in a small native-valid project with helpers.ConversionChecks containing MacroChecks.\n\nA macro calling Context.typeof on an argument containing another static macro also sees the inner call unexpanded: only the outer call was recorded as deferred by the early AST walk. The inner return is inferred from the macro declaration instead of from its expansion. Expanding such calls during typing requires a separate typer engine when the outer engine is already borrowed.\n\nThe module_macro_paths fixture covers a secondary declaration accessed through its module, a normal import, an alias import, nested valid type probes, rejected nested macro arity caught as haxe.macro.Expr.Error, and ordinary static method priority. Native Haxe 4.3.6 --interp passes. Frozen Rayzor v53 aborts on the uncompiled helpers.MacroChecks.describe. The working debug build passes after module aliases, typed static macro lookup and reentrant expansion are added.\n\nThis does not claim that broader Context.typeof validation gaps are solved. For example calling the String returned by a macro still incorrectly type-checks, recorded on 6cd1ac01c7e9322dc033e2d26da15ce956b32611615f82ad6df5cb122e7b7b50.","files":null}]}